{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1563980811,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0NzIzMzc3NzU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3558","origin":"github"},"title":"[wishlist]: rerun --print","message":"NB Please pardon me if there is an issue already - I feel like I have expressed my desires before but can't find it\n\nQuite a few times already I needed to rerun a modified version of previously `datalad run` command.  ATM I see no way to easily obtain a command line invocation I should use/modify from a json record recorded in the history. E.g. what I want is having a record like\n\n```shell\n$\u003e git show\ncommit 7ce688353f1f790964fcb0c0fb5d7cc77301d6ee (HEAD -\u003e master)\nAuthor: Yaroslav Halchenko \u003cdebian@onerussian.com\u003e\nDate:   Wed Jul 24 10:59:34 2019 -0400\n\n    [DATALAD RUNCMD] bash -c 'md5sum urls.csv \u003e MD5SUMS'\n    \n    === Do not change lines below ===\n    {\n     \"chain\": [],\n     \"cmd\": \"bash -c 'md5sum {inputs} \u003e {outputs}'\",\n     \"dsid\": \"9a647e72-ad54-11e9-95c1-8019340ce7f2\",\n     \"exit\": 0,\n     \"extra_inputs\": [],\n     \"inputs\": [\n      \"urls.csv\"\n     ],\n     \"outputs\": [\n      \"MD5SUMS\"\n     ],\n     \"pwd\": \".\"\n    }\n    ^^^ Do not change lines above ^^^\n```\n\nto be able to get something like\n```shell\n$ datalad rerun --print \ndatalad run --explicit --input urls.csv --output MD5SUMS bash -c 'md5sum {inputs} \u003e {outputs}'\n```\nso I could copy/paste and modify to my liking for the next `datalad run` call.\n\nAdditional desires\n- [ ] If original `datalad run` was executed somewhere from a super dataset, inform user about it.  Ideally inspect super datasets to match `dsid` and tell from which path it was/should be ran, e.g.\n\n```shell\n$ datalad rerun --print \ncd ../..\ndatalad run --explicit --input urls.csv --output MD5SUMS bash -c 'md5sum {inputs} \u003e {outputs}'\n```\n\n- [ ] `--print` could either become `--print-cmdline` or become \"smart\" to print Python invocation when invoked from within Python","files":null}]}